  "1": [
   {
    "context": "And by the way, a lot of atheists do that. A lot of people of all sorts of different worldviews. They just trust in whatever their parents told them or whatever the spirit of the ages. They don't get evidence. We ought to get evidence. Let me point out. Well, not completely, but we have really good evidence. It's got to be a spaceless, timeless, immaterial, powerful, personal, intelligent creator.",
    "dist": 0.4104,
    "occurrence_id": 127590,
    "published_at": "2025-02-12T02:00:06Z",
    "role": "host",
    "surface": "evidence",
    "t": 3009.28,
    "title": "[PODCAST] HELP! I Want to Believe! Atheist Woman Shares Her Struggles on the Quest for Truth",
    "video_id": "rPcKnApezU4"
   },
   {
    "context": "And the third type of evidence is personal, involving the acceptance of unlikely, indeed impossible events, and the ascription of the springs of morality to authority, rather than seeking a deeper explanation in terms of our evolutionary and cerebral history.",
    "dist": 0.4133,
    "occurrence_id": 3796,
    "published_at": "2012-04-12T20:33:32Z",
    "role": "host",
    "surface": "evidence",
    "t": 292.72,
    "title": "William Lane Craig vs Peter Atkins (7/8): \"Does God Exist?\", University of Manchester, Oct 2011",
    "video_id": "0smNIQIFSbQ"
   },
   {
    "context": "And I think increasingly so for people in our age. It's interesting that atheists would say there's no evidence. Doesn't that seem to presuppose that this is an orderly universe and the universe is rationally intelligible, that we can find evidence. I mean, not just for God, but for anything. Why can we even ascertain truths outside our skulls? I know our mutual friend, and you've had him on the program many times, John Lennox talks about this quite a bit.",
    "dist": 0.4172,
    "occurrence_id": 36953,
    "published_at": "2025-04-05T01:00:06Z",
    "role": "unknown",
    "surface": "evidence",
    "t": 468.4,
    "title": "[PODCAST] After 20 Years of Debates, Why I'm Still a Christian with @justinbrierley",
    "video_id": "FJli8oe-sHg"
   },
   {
    "context": "You're right. I think so, yeah. It's okay. But you talked about looking at evidence. How can there even be evidence in a world that there is no God? There is no organizer, there is no mind behind all this. Evidence, we wouldn't even exist. Nothing would exist. Reason wouldn't exist unless there's a mind that has given us the laws of reason and minds to know evidence. Respectfully, I think we've jumped a few steps ahead in the argument there.",
    "dist": 0.4198,
    "occurrence_id": 7060,
    "published_at": "2026-04-01T02:32:11Z",
    "role": "host",
    "surface": "evidence",
    "t": 7386.8,
    "title": "'Change My Mind' LIVE Q&A from Ole Miss (Oxford, MS)",
    "video_id": "2FBZ3gpea7I"
   },
   {
    "context": "So in neither case am I appealing to evidence for God. I'm appealing to evidence to show, in the one case, that the universe began to exist, in the other case, that the fine-tuning of the universe is not plausibly explained by chance or physical necessity.",
